{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Subset Construction.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Subset Construction.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"subset construction subset construction","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Subset Construction.md","fileName":"Subset Construction.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Subset Construction","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-16 22:54\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Regular Languages and Finite Automata]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Regular Languages and Finite Automata","Nondeterministic Finite-State Automaton","Deterministic Finite-State Automaton","FoundationsOfComputation_2.3.2.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Subset Construction.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Subset Construction.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"subset construction subset construction","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Subset Construction.md","fileName":"Subset Construction.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Subset Construction","heading":"Subset Construction","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Subset construction converts a [[Nondeterministic Finite-State Automaton]] into a deterministic one by making each DFA state represent a set of NFA states reachable after the same input. Transitions collect every state reachable from the represented set, including those reached through ε-transitions.\n\nThe DFA start state is the NFA start state's ε-closure, and a set-state is accepting when it contains any NFA accepting state. The construction proves that nondeterministic and [[Deterministic Finite-State Automaton|deterministic finite automata]] recognize the same languages.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Regular Languages and Finite Automata","Nondeterministic Finite-State Automaton","Deterministic Finite-State Automaton","FoundationsOfCo